<h2>A short marriage, a long prosecution</h2>
<p>The parties married on 4 February 2016, remained childless, and had lived apart since 2019. The FIR of 2020 alleged misrepresentation about employment and property, cruelty, dowry demands and threats — allegations that, once chargesheeted, would ordinarily have carried the case to trial years after the marriage had effectively ended. The settlement before the Family Court on 16 March 2023 changed the arithmetic: Rs 3,50,000 in full and final settlement of all claims, with divorce by mutual consent under Section 13B.</p>
<p>By the time the quashing petition was heard, Rs 2,50,000 stood paid and the final Rs 1,00,000 demand draft was handed over in court. The wife appeared, confirmed that the settlement was voluntary, and did not oppose quashing. The State raised no objection.</p>

<h2>The legal frame</h2>
<p>Justice Datta applied the settled trilogy — <em>Gian Singh v. State of Punjab</em>, <em>Narinder Singh v. State of Punjab</em> and <em>Jitendra Raghuvanshi v. Babita Raghuvanshi</em> — under which the High Court&rsquo;s inherent power, now housed in Section 528 BNSS, extends to quashing non-compoundable offences where the dispute is predominantly private or matrimonial and a genuine settlement has resolved it. The prosecution here arose from a purely private matrimonial dispute, now genuinely resolved; its continuation would serve no purpose. The FIR and all consequential proceedings were quashed against all petitioners — the in-laws included.</p>

<h2>What the two 5 November quashings show together</h2>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old">
    <h3>Long marriage, children, large corpus</h3>
    <p>In the companion decision of the same day, a 2013 marriage with two sons closed with a Rs 1.5 crore settlement, Rs 1 crore earmarked for the children, custody resolved, and costs imposed.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="arrow">&rarr;</div>
  <div class="col new">
    <h3>Short marriage, no children, modest sum</h3>
    <p>Here a 2016 marriage, childless and separated since 2019, closed at Rs 3,50,000 with no costs. The same doctrine scaled to very different facts.</p>
  </div>
</div>
<p>The comparison is instructive because quantum anxiety dominates settlement negotiations. What the High Court actually polices is different: voluntariness, performance, and the genuinely private character of the dispute. A settlement proportioned to the marriage — its length, its dependants, its assets — passes muster at any size.</p>

<h2>Practice pointers</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p>Record the settlement before a forum — the Family Court here — rather than leaving it as a private MOU; the judicial record anchors the later quashing petition.</p>
  <p>Complete the Section 13B process before seeking quashing; a dissolved marriage is the strongest evidence that the dispute is truly over.</p>
  <p>Stage payments so the final instalment coincides with the quashing hearing, and bring the draft to court.</p>
  <p>Ensure the complainant appears — her in-person confirmation of voluntariness is what the Court acts on.</p>
  <p>Name every accused in the settlement; quashing qua the in-laws depends on the settlement covering them in terms.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">For conduct after 1 July 2024, the cruelty offence corresponds to Section 85 of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023, and the inherent jurisdiction is exercised under Section 528 BNSS — as it was in this very petition, though the FIR itself predated the new codes.</div>
